joined today by dr. Karen DeSalvo who  has practiced medicine for 20 years in  Louisiana and most recently served as  health commissioner in New Orleans and  then as acting assistant secretary for  health in the Obama administration until  January of 2017 it's so great to talk to  you you know we've been following the  various iterations of Republican  attempts to repeat a repeal and replace  the Affordable Care Act  each worse than the previous in some  ways with some marginal improvements  depending on where you look from a sort  of 30,000 foot perspective at this point  what do you think is the fundamental  problem with the approach that  Republicans are taking to these bills  it's hard to me that state 30,000 feet  cannot be honest with you all I can see  are the faces of the patients that I  took care of over the years who are  uninsured and who now I know are going  to have to struggle again go to bed  worried that they're not going to be  able to get the medicine they need or to  have some bankruptcy issues I mean this  is also not just about the end in short  but I think the fundamental challenge  that we're facing with this new approach  is that it touches everybody in this  country in some way it stands to reverse  some tremendous progress that we've made  in last eight years to see that people  have access to affordable health  insurance which gives them access to  care and then health and so it's a  pretty big step backwards one of the the  approaches I've taken is that if you  start with Obamacare which is really no  progressive panacea right I mean we're  talking about particularly when we talk  about the the individual mandate this is  something that was proposed by a  conservative think tank during the 1990s  there's nothing socialistic in any way  of mandating that tens of millions of  people buy products from for-profit  companies when you start with that and  then you look at the promises that were  made by the current president of it's  going to cost less no one will lose  coverage and quality of care won't go  down it's just sort of impossible right  I mean there's there's really no way  that you can take Obama care which is  already not as I said far to the left  and go further right and do those things  that the  said can be done somewhere someone's  either going to have to pay more or have  fewer benefits or have a lack of access  it from a mathematical standpoint am I  missing something there well you know  you're exactly right the Affordable Care  Act proposal 1 was built upon principles  that were considered to be an inter  middle-of-the-road to build on the  capitalist system that we had of private  insurance and the private sector being  able to deliver care as well as for  shoring up the safety net and over time  to get people to have independence  financially to be able to shop for their  care so they have their own insurance  product that would allow competition in  the marketplace from from the delivery  system so a lot of free-market  principles built in but then a  recognition that for a variety of  reasons that health insurance is unabled  for many people and we have to be  attentive to this idea that people walk  into the insurance marketplace with  pre-existing conditions I think there's  also an interesting part about the  Affordable Care Act that a lot of people  forget which is it freed people from  needing to have employer-sponsored  insurance or stranded freed young people  up to the age of 26 need to be able to  have coverage so it changed not just the  way we provided care for low income  people changed the way all of us receive  insurance about you know pre-existing  conditions and the affordability of the  products or the way that that we can  anticipate those going to cover mental  health screening prevention and and so  all that of course was expected to cost  more and honestly it was moving along  the pathway as the budgetary  expectations were wasn't bringing more  people into the system but even within  with bringing more people into the  system the Affordable Care Act was able  to bend the cost curve in this is really  important because what we were able to  do and still are is to see that care is  not just available to more people but  it's more efficient and this is this  discussion about polling coverage from  people distracts from that opportunity  to actually make care more  person-centered and more efficient more  efficient for people because at the end  of the day we all agree I think that we  need to be more thoughtful about how  much care cost it doesn't mean we can we  should do that by taking it away from  people we should just make it more  efficient yeah and there's some broader  conversations there  in terms of should-should health  insurance companies if we're going to  have the type of system that we have  which I don't believe is the best system  but if we're going to have it should  there be health insurance reimbursements  for outcomes or for each procedure and  test and thing that is done by a care  provider that's sort of a different  conversation one thing I do want want to  ask you because of of the work that  you're doing now I in my mind have this  initial reaction when I hear twenty two  million people are going to lose  coverage even if we take out the ones  that will sort of lose it because they  don't want it right some people will no  longer be forced to buy insurance and  will choose not to but if we forget  about that we forget about those who  will be priced out because of the  increased cost if you have pre-existing  conditions or for all of the other  reasons do they in your mind understand  why that would happen in other words  will there be political repercussions to  those who repeal and replace Obamacare  or will it just be blamed on Democrats  as Republicans and Donald Trump have  tried to do for months now will will  there be a price to pay I guess is what  I'm asking if indeed millions of people  lose coverage so two things on that and  one is that the price to pay clearly is  in the health of people so no matter the  political repercussions as a doctor as  somebody who has been in this space for  a long time the data is pretty clear  that health insurance saves lives and it  reduces suffering that there is going to  be suffering and people probably are  going to lose their life if they don't  have health insurance coverage and  that's not I think the second piece of  this that folks forget is that most of  the people who have benefited from what  happened under the Affordable Care Act  from an insurance standpoint are people  who are working people who are working  poor in particular that have no no way  to exit access to health insurance  coverage and make too much even to be  eligible in many states for Medicaid and  maybe I just add a third thing as we  keep getting hung up on this 22 million  number and they're very importantly  people they're going to be you know our  patients and our neighbors and our  family who are going to lose  access to coverage but the kinds of  changes that are being thought about  have repercussions that will touch  everybody in this country so you know  remember that for example more than half  of nursing home care is paid for by the  Medicaid program that half of the babies  in this country are born into into the  Medicaid program so this is not a small  proportion of our population that would  be that would be impacted if we make  these kinds of financial choices in  front of health choices and I would just  really like the conversation to stay  focused on what this means for the  health and well-being of our communities  and the people that we know and love in  our communities including for  documenting our patients are you of the  mindset that the employer connected  for-profit health insurance paradigm  that we have can be quote fixed in a way  that really works well or do we need to  be looking at other systems altogether  like most of our other developed country  counterparts have now I'll actually  point to the American Medical  Association who in their recent Congress  added a need to look at other models of  financing health care including  single-payer systems so even kind of  traditionally conservative physician  groups are starting to say maybe we  should at least look at the option the  reality is is that we have a private pay  system by large that has some overlap  with a public pay system it's worked  mostly it's been working increasingly  better for the last eight years as more  people have been brought into the system  so I frankly would rather see us in the  short run fix what's wrong with the  current system that we have find ways to  make sure that their choices of products  in the marketplace that there are that  we don't have challenges with  out-of-pocket costs for people but they  actually can get access especially for  treatments for chronic disease when they  have it so there are plenty of ways that  would get sick even a current system  wherein all we at the same time in the  country think about what's the most  affordable but most effective way to  provide to provide coverage in the long  term yeah and that's one of those things  where when I hear that you know on the  right they laugh and say hey premiums  continue to increase under Obamacare and  of course that's true but they've  arguably increased less quickly than  they are  their lies would have I think it's  important to fact-check that when we  hear that from the right but at the same  time if the victory is well yes it keeps  getting more expensive but not by as  much as it otherwise might have it still  feels like it's a it's a sort of Pyrrhic  victory in a way it's a victory but  we're still not fixing the overarching  problem well I mean we've been we've  been talking about fixing health care  for a long time and it is creeping up to  being moving for me a fix of our economy  to assist of our 